                 r"\textbf{Today's reach}: %s%s; in the thorium nuclear clock, whose line is between $%s$ and $%s$ kHz wide today, the bound stands $%s$ orders away --- the v200's $%s$ orders were the projection of a coherence thorium does not have yet. Among all platforms, the one with the largest reach for an $\omega^2$ law is %s, at $%s$ orders (%s). Read the other way, in the per-particle reading the data bound the scale: from clocks, $\tau_\star\le %s$ Planck times (95 per cent bound); from the platform with the largest reach (%s), $\tau_\star\le %s$ (%s). Five standard deviations with today's clock would require superposing states at least about $%s$ MeV apart. "
